# Line endings, pinned rather than left to the machine.
#
# This repository is configured with core.autocrlf=true, so without the rules
# below git rewrites text files to CRLF on checkout. Two things then break, and
# both were measured rather than predicted:
#
#   gofmt refuses a CRLF Go file, so test.ps1 fails at its first step on a file
#   nobody had edited. It happened after a commit on 2026-09-22 and named six
#   files at once.
#
#   A shell script checked out with CRLF has `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` as its
#   first line, and Linux looks for an interpreter whose name ends in a
#   carriage return. The Flatpak and DMG builders would fail on the platform
#   they exist for.
#
# Pinning is the fix rather than normalising by hand each time: a rule that has
# to be remembered after every checkout is not a rule.

# Let git decide what is text, then store every text file with LF.
* text=auto

# The files where the ending is load-bearing, stated outright.
*.go text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# PowerShell is only ever run on Windows here, and CRLF is what it expects.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The Go toolchain rewrites go.mod and go.sum with LF whatever it finds, so
# without these two they check out as CRLF and then read as modified with no
# content change the first time any go command touches them. Measured on
# 2026-09-23, when go.mod sat in git status through a whole session saying
# nothing.
*.mod text eol=lf
*.sum text eol=lf

# Anything git might mistake for text.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.db binary
